# THE CHECKSUM FLYWAY RECORDED FOR EVERY MIGRATION, SO THAT EDITING ONE STOPS HERE RATHER THAN ON
# THE CONTOUR. Flyway stores a checksum per applied version and refuses to start when the file no
# longer matches — the init container then crash-loops and the deploy rolls back on a timeout,
# which reads as a slow rollout rather than as an edit.
#
# It happened, to a COMMENT. V11 was deployed, then its comment was corrected — no SQL changed, and
# the checksum is over the whole file — and the next release could not start. Recovery needs
# `flyway repair` against every contour that already ran it.
#
# ADDING A LINE IS ROUTINE; CHANGING ONE IS THE DEFECT THIS FILE EXISTS TO CATCH. If a number here
# has to move, the migration has already run somewhere and you owe every one of those places a
# repair — a new migration is nearly always the cheaper answer, comments included.
#
# The arithmetic is not trusted: `MigrationChecksumOracleTest` runs real Flyway against a real
# Postgres and asserts these numbers are the ones it writes into `flyway_schema_history`.
V1__petich_storage.sql -2045143830
V2__subscriber_account_esim.sql -273996047
V3__otp_challenge.sql -1259623264
V4__session_family.sql 786084059
V5__purchase.sql 1600772439
